## 11. Parallel-track pattern (successful precedent: Sprint v0.8.1)

Sprint v0.8.1 (Hierarchy + E2E + Demo Clean) ran as 3 parallel tracks
that merged cleanly:

| Track | Branch | Scope | Issues | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `sprint-v0.8.1/track-1-hierarchy` | Hierarchy + RLS | #516-#524 | 8 approved + 1 approved_with_followups |
| 2 | `sprint-v0.8.1/track-2-structural` | Break-glass + honest audit + data products | #525-#529 | 5 approved |
| 3 | `sprint-v0.8.1/track-3-e2e` | E2E + demo | #530-#534 | 2 approved + 3 approved_with_followups |

**Integration model:** Track 3 merged Track 1 and Track 2 into itself in
sequence (Track 1 → Track 3 first, then Track 2 → Track 3). The final
integration branch `sprint-v0.8.1-integration` was just a rename of the
fully-merged Track 3. One migration-number collision (Track 1's
`audit_log_rls_outcome` had taken 051; Track 2's `break_glass_audit` was
also numbered 051) was resolved at integration time by renumbering
Track 2's migrations 052-056. The rename was mechanical because
`migrations/` files are independent units.

**Why it worked:**

- **Migrations partitioned by intent, not by author.** Track 1 owned
  hierarchy + RLS migrations (045-051), Track 2 owned audit + dev-tools
  + registry migrations (initially 051-055, renumbered to 052-056).
  When tracks collided at the same migration number, the resolution was
  obvious — the structural piece moved.
- **Tests partitioned by file.** Each track wrote `tests/test_*.py` for
  its own contract. No two tracks edited the same test file.
- **Single Django app per track.** Track 1 owned `apps/audit`, Track 2
  owned `apps/audit` extensions (break_glass.py, dev_elevate_session),
  Track 3 owned `apps/registry` + `apps/transforms` + `apps/platform_admin`.
  The one apps/audit overlap was resolved by Track 2 explicitly building
  on top of Track 1's `rls_middleware.py` rather than rewriting it.
- **Each track wrote its own completion audits.** Track 2 wrote
  `plans/525-529-completion-audit.json` before merging; Track 3 wrote
  `plans/530-534-completion-audit.json`; the sprint-close pass wrote
  `plans/516-524-completion-audit.json` retroactively after audit-tool
  was available.

**When not to use this pattern:**

- If two tracks need to edit `settings.py` or `docker-compose.yml`,
  serialize them.
- If a migration carries a destructive ALTER (DROP COLUMN, ALTER TYPE),
  the migration must be a single track's responsibility — no parallel
  rewriter.
- If two tracks both write to `audit_log` schema, integration order
  matters — the track that adds new columns must merge first, the
  track that renames must merge second.

